     1	# Proposed successor expression contract
     2	
     3	Status: **PROPOSAL — SP01.3 expression layer only.** Contract identifier
     4	`moriarty-expression-contract/1`; this is not a registered execution profile.
     5	It does not replace `moriarty-funded-source/0`, the syntax profile, or archived
     6	candidate04. No full RP01, SP02, SP03, theorem, signature or Midnight acceptance
     7	follows from this document. The Boolean design change has two scoped independent
     8	votes; these exact revised source bytes still require fresh GPT-6/Grok result
     9	reviews. The 38 proposed financial operations are not defined here.
    10	
    11	This document and [static-semantics.md](static-semantics.md) give the meaning of
    12	all 40 expression constructors declared in candidate04 B.1. The complete index
    13	is [expression-signatures.json](expression-signatures.json). The exact mathematical
    14	JSON embedding and every byte/node aggregate are defined by
    15	[representation.md](representation.md); no implementation may choose another
    16	layout for these semantic bounds. Structural validation
    17	of that index is distinct from the [specified derivation cases](expression-cases.json).
    18	The documents, not the structural validator, define the proposed semantics.
    19	
    20	## Authority and decisions
    21	
    22	Inherited requirements: immutable pre-state; sequential immutable locals;
    23	one tentative write per field; no `next` reads; post-state available only in the
    24	final `ensures` suffix; first lexical failed clause; financial rollback; finite
    25	work; distinct nominal debt and tokens; no host flag establishes acceptance.
    26	Sources: [LANGUAGE-DESIGN](../../../../deliverables/defi-language-design-2026-09-07/LANGUAGE-DESIGN.md:46),
    27	[SP01.3](../../../../openspec/sprints/sp01-financial-contract-and-execution-admission.md:49),
    28	[SP03](../../../../openspec/sprints/sp03-executable-bounded-semantics-in-k.md:39).
    29	
    30	The following are **new proposed choices**, not inherited approvals:
    31	
    32	| ID | Choice and reason |
    33	| --- | --- |
    34	| EX-D1 | Left-to-right short-circuit `And`/`Or`, matching the source design. Both operands are statically checked and all supplied snapshots admitted before reduction; only the selected right operand is evaluated. This deliberately replaces the strict EX-D1 of contract/0 under a distinct contract/1 identifier. |
    35	| EX-D2 | Checked same-type scalar arithmetic, indexed amount/share addition and subtraction, explicit quantity scale/unit arithmetic. No implicit asset, scale, width or debt conversion. This makes overflow and rounding visible. |
    36	| EX-D3 | `ReadPre` has a closed view operand `pre|post`; `post` is legal only in Ensure's condition. No new 41st constructor and no `ReadNext` are introduced. Read-view is part of Core encoding, never inferred from a name. |
    37	| EX-D4 | Every entered node costs one ordinary work unit. Finite helper operations do not each count as nodes. Recovery work is unchanged here. Failed preparation reports consumed local work, but publishes no candidate financial state/effects. This is a proposed local cost semantics, not native proof cost or authority consumption. |
    38	| EX-D5 | AccessField/AccessIndex are exact aliases of ProjectField/ProjectIndex, including errors and work. Core preserves their names; an optimizer may not delete their node charge without a separate correspondence argument. |
    39	| EX-D6 | Emit appends a typed operation descriptor only. Effects and financial state changes require the separately defined financial-operation transition. There is no default Transfer, repayment or other execution guessed from the descriptor. |
    40	| EX-D7 | Finite parameters below are proposal limits, independent of existing parser/funded bounds. UTF-8 spans are half-open byte offsets. Whole-action static rejection precedes any execution. |
    41	| EX-D8 | Unit is statement-result-only; schema fields are classified ordinary/financial. Raw NextWrite cannot bypass financial transitions. These are proposed type/registry mechanisms enforcing the inherited no-debt-erasure requirement. |
    42	
    43	The source proposal selects short-circuit `and`/`or`
    44	([line71](../../../../deliverables/defi-language-design-2026-09-07/LANGUAGE-DESIGN.md:71)).
    45	This revision gives Core And/Or that same choice. The [reviewed decision](../../../../deliverables/sp01-surface-core-contract-2026-09-10/scoped-design-approval-01.json)
    46	approves the design, not these exact implementation bytes or full source/Core
    47	correspondence. Contract/0, its strict rejection cases and original approvals
    48	remain retrievable through the [preservation map](../../../../deliverables/sp01-surface-core-contract-2026-09-10/preservation-map-02.json).
    49	A compiler, signature or proof bound to the strict lineage cannot silently opt
    50	into /1. Source hashes and eventual semantic profile hashes remain distinct.
    51	
    52	The AMM fragment still needs mixed Amount products/division absent from this
    53	proposed overload table; those requirements remain open, not removed by
    54	TYPE_MISMATCH here.
    55	
    56	Unresolved privacy, observation authentication, operation effects and signing
    57	policy are explicit external obligations. Mathematical values below do not
    58	establish permission to disclose a value, condition, error or state. A future
    59	public execution profile must bind its confidentiality and observation policy;
    60	this proposal cannot accept a private workflow by omitting that policy.
    61	
    62	## V — finite domains and values
    63	
    64	A candidate binds a finite schema environment Σ and input snapshot before
    65	checking the action. No host callback, arbitrary JSON, mutable object reference,
    66	user function, recursion or implicit cast is a Core value.
    67	
    68	Proposed simultaneous limits: 65,536 source UTF-8 bytes; 4,096 AST nodes;
    69	64 nesting depth; 256 declarations and 256 statements; 64 fields per record;
    70	128 members per collection/enum; 1,024 UTF-8 bytes per Text; identifiers match
    71	`[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,63}`; at most 65,536 canonical UTF-8 bytes and 4,096
    72	nodes in each complete input value or assembled candidate state; at most 128
    73	operation descriptors, each within the same value limits; work 0..65,536.
    74	A value node is one scalar or compound value constructor; names, type metadata
    75	and tags are not additional value nodes. A compound contributes one plus its
    76	child value nodes, counting repeated occurrences separately. AST nodes count
    77	entered constructor occurrences, not metadata or list containers.
    78	
    79	All aggregate collections, including Σ, arguments, observations and pre-state,
    80	are checked against those byte/node/depth bounds as complete records, not only
    81	against per-field bounds. Σ type definitions form an acyclic graph. Cardinalities
    82	must fit simultaneously: products of individual maxima do not grant larger
    83	aggregate limits. Inputs failing a bound never enter evaluation.
    84	
    85	These maxima are proposed contract parameters motivated by the retained bounded
    86	proposal and parser. They have no native fit, runtime performance or usability
    87	claim. Changing a maximum changes this contract's eventual profile digest.
    88	
    89	Values have exact types, recursively:
    90	
    91	- `UInt64`: 0..2^64−1; `UInt128`: 0..2^128−1;
    92	  `SInt128`: −2^127..2^127−1; `Bool`. `Unit` is a statement-result marker only,
    93	  not a storable, argument, observation, field or collection/option element type.
    94	- `Text`: a well-formed Unicode scalar sequence whose UTF-8 is at most 1,024
    95	  bytes. No normalization; comparison is exact decoded scalar equality.
    96	- `Amount<A>`: UInt128 quanta indexed by a declared asset identifier A.
    97	- `Shares<V,H>`: UInt128 share quanta indexed by a declared vault V and holder H.
    98	- `Rate<S>`: signed SInt128 mantissa with type-level scale S, 0..18; value m/10^S.
    99	- `Price<A,B,S>`: UInt128 mantissa, declared distinct base A and quote B,
   100	  scale S, direction fixed as base-per-quote; value m/10^S A per B.
   101	  In particular Price<AssetB,AssetA,4>(19743) is 1.9743 AssetB per AssetA,
   102	  matching the retained swap convention; parameter order is not interchangeable.
   103	- `Quantity<U,S>`: SInt128 mantissa with scale S and a canonical unit vector U over the separate declared Σ.units roster.
   104	  U contains at most eight distinct identifier/exponent pairs in increasing ASCII
   105	  identifier order. Exponents are nonzero integers −128..127. An empty vector
   106	  is dimensionless. Values denote m/10^S times the product of symbol powers.
   107	- `Record<R>`: every field of the declared record R exactly once, with its
   108	  declared type. Field names are sorted by ASCII identifier for value encoding;
   109	  source constructor operands retain lexical order for evaluation.
   110	- `Enum<E>`: one member of declared enum E. Payload enums are not implicit;
   111	  use a record plus an option when required.
   112	- `Option<T>`: `None<T>` or `Some<T>(v:T)`.
   113	- `Collection<T,N>`: ordered values all of T, length 0..N, 0≤N≤128. Capacity is
   114	  in the type; equal lengths do not make different capacities/types equal.
   115	- `Operation<O>`: descriptor `{operation:O, fields:Record<R_O>}` for a declared
   116	  operation O with exact operand schema R_O. It contains no executable callback.
   117	  This descriptor is formed by Emit; it is not a ledger effect.
   118	
   119	Record schemas may encode financial identities and debt records, but these
   120	expressions confer no authority to create/change a financial resource. Persistent
   121	field registry entries include `writeClass=ordinary|financial`; only ordinary
   122	fields may be targeted by NextWrite. Financial fields are read-only at this
   123	layer. A profile must define the complete financial operation relation and
   124	reconcile its footprint with staged ordinary writes before financial execution.
   125	This prevents a raw next-write from erasing debt or bypassing cash effects.
   126	
   127	Core metadata comprises identifiers, integers, type references, record field
   128	names, enum members and read views. It is bounded, not executable. Numeric
   129	metadata and values use canonical decimal strings: unsigned `0|[1-9][0-9]*`;
   130	signed adds `-[1-9][0-9]*`. No `+`, leading zeros or `-0`. Booleans use JSON
   131	booleans. Each Core node is a closed tagged record with its named operands and
   132	span, no extra keys. No duplicate JSON keys. Canonical serialization uses sorted
   133	object keys, ordered arrays, UTF-8 with no BOM, no insignificant whitespace and
   134	no Unicode normalization; escaping is JSON minimal (quote/backslash/control
   135	characters escaped, other scalar characters emitted). Quote/backslash use their
   136	short JSON escapes; backspace, form-feed, newline, carriage-return and tab use
   137	\b, \f, \n, \r and \t respectively; other U+0000..U+001F use lowercase
   138	\u00xx. This fixes the escaping choice. Any future wire codec
   139	must implement and test this; the structural checker is not that codec.
   140	
   141	## E — evaluation contexts and frames
   142	
   143	The configuration is `⟨control, pre, writes, locals, args, obs, descriptors,
   144	workInitial, workRemaining, result⟩`. Pre/args/obs/Σ are immutable finite snapshots.
   145	Writes and locals begin empty; descriptors begin empty. Existing duties,
   146	authority and recovery reserve are part of the supplied state, not fresh budget.
   147	Snapshots are assumed supplied consistently; this layer does not authenticate them.
   148	
   149	Distinguish an unentered constructor occurrence from a returned value. Literal
   150	syntax is a constructor occurrence; a returned Boolean value is not a fresh
   151	LitBool node. Every occurrence retains its original nodePath and span. Write
   152	`K°` for a constructor already entered; the circle is administrative machine
   153	notation, not a new Core constructor, value, encoding tag or work unit.
   154	
   155	The evaluation contexts are:
   156	
   157	```text
   158	C ::= []
   159	    | K°(v1,…,v(i−1),C,e(i+1),…,en)    K is not And or Or
   160	    | And°(C,e2)
   161	    | Or°(C,e2)
   162	```
   163	
   164	The generic K alternative ranges over its signature's **evaluatedOperands** in
   165	listed order, expanded in lexical list order. Metadata is not evaluated. For
   166	Let/NextWrite only the value is evaluated; Require/Ensure only the condition.
   167	Records/lists retain lexical operand order, never canonical field order.
   168	For And/Or, `evaluatedOperands=[left]` and `conditionalEvaluatedOperands` names
   169	the right operand with its selection predicate. Both still occur in the syntax
   170	and static traversal. Child indices count original expression operands, so
   171	left is p+[0] and right is p+[1] even when the right is skipped. There is no
   172	generic And°(v,C) or Or°(v,C) alternative.
   173	
   174	Small steps are deterministic proposals:
   175	
   176	1. On attempting to enter an occurrence with work=0, reject `WORK_EXHAUSTED` at
   177	   that occurrence without consuming work or entering children. Otherwise
   178	   decrement work once and mark it entered. Resuming a context never recharges it.
   179	2. Enter children through the contexts above. The first rejection discards the
   180	   entire continuation, tentative writes, locals and descriptors.
   181	3. For K other than And/Or, apply its rule after all evaluated children return
   182	   values. Primitive failure uses K's original span/path. For the entered Boolean
   183	   nodes use the four administrative contractions below after the left returns.
   184	4. A statement's success advances to the next lexical statement. Ensure clauses
   185	   are a suffix and see `post=pre overridden by writes`; that view does not commit.
   186	5. After all statements/ensures pass, check aggregate candidate encoding bounds
   187	   and return `ExpressionPrepared(post,descriptors,workRemaining)`.
   188	
   189	The Boolean contractions are:
   190	
   191	```text
   192	And°(false,e2) -> false
   193	And°(true, e2) -> e2
   194	Or°(true, e2)  -> true
   195	Or°(false,e2)  -> e2
   196	```
   197	
   198	Each contraction costs zero work. A skipped case returns the already obtained
   199	left value and creates no chargeable literal. A selected case resumes the
   200	original right occurrence at p+[1], with that child's original span, even though
   201	the And/Or control node is removed. An enclosing context retains its own original
   202	provenance for a later parent failure. Selecting a child does not copy it, rebase
   203	it to p, or cause a second And/Or entry. If the remaining work is zero, a skipped
   204	case can finish; a selected unentered child rejects WORK_EXHAUSTED at p+[1].
   205	Left-child failures retain p+[0] plus their nested path; the right is not entered.
   206	
   207	Let C(e) be actual ordinary work on a successful pure expression, and let B(e)
   208	count one for every constructor occurrence in the whole syntax, including
   209	unselected branches:
   210	
   211	```text
   212	C(And(e1,e2)) = 1 + C(e1) + (C(e2) if e1 returns true  else 0)
   213	C(Or(e1,e2))  = 1 + C(e1) + (C(e2) if e1 returns false else 0)
   214	B(K(e1,…,en)) = 1 + sum_i B(ei)
   215	B(And(e1,e2)) = B(Or(e1,e2)) = 1 + B(e1) + B(e2)
   216	```
   217	
   218	Read/literal nodes have B=1; metadata and array/field wrappers add no work.
   219	For a failed evaluation, count only its entered-node prefix, not a missing entry
   220	or an unselected suffix. B is finite under the simultaneous source/Core bounds
   221	and conservatively bounds this local reduction work; it does not estimate
   222	validation overhead, lifecycle work or native proof cost. Admission and complete
   223	action typing are unchanged, and no skip can waive an aggregate limit or a
   224	registered conservative static-bound ceiling. No registered profile is changed
   225	here.
   226	
   227	The Boolean-only source/Core mapping is `a and b` to `And(core(a),core(b))` and
   228	`a or b` to `Or(core(a),core(b))`, once per source operator. The existing source
   229	left associativity gives `a and b and c` the shape `And(And(a,b),c)`. Original
   230	source spans must be retained. No If expansion, thunk, host callback or duplicate
   231	evaluation is introduced. This equation specifies the Boolean mapping; a full
   232	elaborator, parser-to-Core trace and correspondence proof remain unimplemented.
   233	
   234	Result is **ExpressionPrepared**, never accepted `Prepared`, `Complete` or
   235	`Pending`. It is not financially executable without the operation-layer rule.
   236	For a descriptor-free ordinary action it specifies a tentative state only.
   237	An external financial rule must consume descriptors, account for its own work,
   238	produce complete ordered effects, preserve duties and resolve postcondition
   239	visibility before the combined language can execute. This missing composition
   240	is not silently given an evaluation order here.
   241	
   242	Rejection is `Rejected(code, span, nodePath, workUsed)` with no post-state or
   243	financial effects; workUsed is local attempted reduction cost, not a committed
   244	ledger/authority charge. Input admission/static rejection has workUsed=0.
   245	Runtime workUsed=workInitial−workRemaining includes the entered failing node
   246	unless failure is inability to enter it. No failed preparation increases a
   247	stored work budget or permits an on-chain retry. Continuing, charging a failure
   248	or using recovery work belongs to the future lifecycle/acceptance relation.
   249	
   250	Span `[start,end)` is UTF-8 byte offsets, 0≤start≤end≤sourceBytes. Elaboration must
   251	retain real node spans; synthetic Core has explicit synthetic spans and cannot
   252	claim a source diagnostic. For a rejection, use the first offending node's span only when it is a valid P
   253	for the supplied source: closed shape, canonical integer offsets, ordered and
   254	in range, or synthetic with both offsets zero. If that span is absent, malformed
   255	or out of range, return `{kind:synthetic,start:0,end:0}` (with decimal-string
   256	offsets in the presentation tree). Keep the original offending nodePath and
   257	workUsed; do not clamp the malformed range, mutate the input, or erase its path.
   258	In particular INPUT_SPAN cannot copy an invalid input P into Rejected.span.
   259	Synthetic spans must be explicitly
   260	marked and exactly `[0,0)`; they cannot fabricate source offsets. `nodePath` is an ordered child-index
   261	path from the action root; statement index is its first element. For an isolated
   262	expression derivation, its root path is empty and children begin at index zero. For post-final
   263	aggregate failure use the action's span/path. No wrapping private data in errors.
   264	
   265	## R — primitive reduction rules
   266	
   267	The exact rules and all overloads appear in the companion static contract.
   268	Common rules apply to all 40 nodes: admission and typing first; entering/child
   269	ordering above; no mutation by pure expressions; no implicit helper effects;
   270	checked finite result before returning. Integer checks compare mathematical
   271	integers to the declared bounds and never wrap. A multiplication checks its
   272	product before any subsequent division, even when the final quotient would fit.
   273	Literal/metadata validity is checked statically; runtime snapshot equality and
   274	value domain checks occur during whole-input admission, not arbitrary late reads.
   275	
   276	Post-state fields not written retain their exact pre-state values, including
   277	financial records and identities. No integer debt subtraction is a transfer.
   278	Only the operation-layer contract can authorize discharge/write-off/migration;
   279	RequestFulfill/Claim, MessageRefund, RewardAccrue/Claim and EventClaimSettle
   280	remain underdetermined in candidate04. This file invents no payout or funding rule.
   281	
   282	## Open interfaces and theorem scope
   283	
   284	The archived signing proposal's 38 financial rows, genesis funding, full
   285	contextual negative histories, cancellation backing and display identity fixes
   286	remain open. This 40-constructor document does not reconcile the full RP01
   287	challenge map or vote on an accepted base-Core membership list.
   288	
   289	A later combined contract must resolve financial-operation execution versus
   290	ordinary staged writes and postconditions, authenticated/labelled observations,
   291	privacy and disclosure, canonical codec implementation, lifecycle/authority work
   292	and acceptance statuses. Until then, a full successor runtime must reject an
   293	attempt to execute these missing interfaces; descriptor preparation alone cannot
   294	be exposed as a completed financial action.
   295	
   296	The six SP03.3 obligations remain determinism, progress-or-defined-rejection,
   297	type preservation, finite termination, residual-duty conservation and
   298	elaboration/evaluator correspondence **for the frozen base domain**. This source
   299	contract and its derivations are not mechanized proofs. No K proof or frontend
   300	correspondence is performed here. The existing Transfer/Repay evaluator, numeric
   301	K evidence and compiled Midnight loan/swap evidence retain their original scope.

